  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure sits to our east this morning, with mostly clear\nskies to start the day. Clouds will gradually increase from west\nto east as a system passes to our north and south. The first\nwave will bring a chance for light rain showers to northeast PA\nthis afternoon, while a second wave brings additional showers \nto north central NY this evening. Isolated showers may also \ndevelop across the Finger Lakes and portions of the Southern \nTier between these features. The better chance for widespread \nrain showers arrives late tonight into Monday morning as low \npressure drags a cold front through the region. Cold air \nadvection behind the front will then allow lake effect rain \nshowers to develop across north central NY Monday. These showers\nmay linger into the early afternoon before high pressure \nbuilding in from the west brings drier conditions.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nHigh pressure settles back into the region Monday night,\nbringing northwesterly flow and a cooler air mass. With 850 mb\ntemperatures falling to around 0 degrees C, highs Monday and \nTuesday will only reach the 50s to low 60s. Overnight lows\nMonday and Tuesday night will fall into the mid to upper 30s, \nwith portions of northern Oneida county dropping below freezing.\nFrost has been introduced across much of the area, with these \ntwo nights potentially brining the first widespread frost of the\nfall season. By Wednesday morning, the ridge axis and surface \nhigh will shift east of the region, allowing southerly flow to \nreturn and usher in warmer more moist air. Temperatures should \nreturn to near normal for the remainder of the week, with highs \ngenerally in the 60s and lows in the 40s.",
